#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147158 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4e3079d-80a6-5333-8304-7116b01de82c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Constant Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "40068330-4d79-501b-afbc-a96f8e210c37" :question/math-academy-id "e-21598" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nF(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether\n\n$$\ns \\neq 0\n$$\n\nor $s = 0$.\n\n- If $s \\neq 0$, we have\n$F(s)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}) \\mid _{0}^{a}; =-\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1)$.\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n$lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}s > 0, \\\\ ∞,\\end{vmatrix}s < 0$.\n- If $s = 0$, we have\n$F(0)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{-0t}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)t \\mid _{0}^{a}; = 2lim_(a → ∞)(a - 0); = 2lim_(a → ∞)a; = ∞$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n- For $s > 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is convergent\n- For $s < 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= -\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{s}(0 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147159 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2fd0466-9f47-5adc-911a-f297858951b1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Real Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "54b1f55e-6525-557c-9dbc-869264259455" :question/math-academy-id "e-21599" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t}$." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-2t}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st - 2t}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether\n\n$$\ns \\neq -2\n$$\n\nor $s = -2$.\n\n- If $s \\neq -2$, we have\n$F(s)|= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{1}{-2 - s}e^{(-2 - s)t}) \\mid _{0}^{a}; = \\frac{1}{-2 - s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} - 1); = \\frac{1}{s + 2}(1 - lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a}); = \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a}$\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n$lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}s > - 2, \\\\ ∞,\\end{vmatrix}s < - 2$.\n- If $s = -2$, we have\n$F(-2)|= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - (-2))t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{0t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)t \\mid _{0}^{a}; = lim_(a → ∞)(a - 0); = lim_(a → ∞)a; = ∞$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s \\leq -2$ the integral is divergent.\n- For $s > -2$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s > -2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s > -2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147160 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a90a0c43-d87f-5639-826d-854b90876a27" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Complex Exponentials"}] :question/id #uuid "92327ab7-0ae5-54ce-92d1-a3365407dd74" :question/math-academy-id "e-21602" :question/problem "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{2\\textrm i t}$, where $t, s \\in\\mathbb R$?" :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{2\\textrm i t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}e^{2\\textrm{i}t}dt = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{1}{2\\textrm{i} - s}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}) \\mid _{0}^{a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\textrm{i} - s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)a} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}(1 - lim_(a → ∞)e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)a}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} \\cdot e^{2\\textrm{i}a})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)= \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i} - \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\\left[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty}e^{-sa}\\cdot \\left(\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)\\right) \\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s < 0$, the term $e^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s < 0$.\n- For $s = 0$, we have simply $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$, which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s = 0$.\n- For $s > 0$, the term $e^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s > 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}[lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} \\cdot (\\cos (2a) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2a))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $e^{2\\textrm i t}$ is\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0$."}]}
